TEXT 2304 2488 Left 2 !.inc CD4000_v.lib
TEXT 2616 2552 Left 2 !.param VDD1=3V
TEXT 2304 2512 Left 2 !.inc CD4011B.cir
TEXT 2304 2536 Left 2 !.inc CD4071B.cir
TEXT 2304 2560 Left 2 !.inc CD4081B.cir
TEXT 2616 2528 Left 2 !.tran 0 18000s 0 2ms startup
TEXT 704 -2600 Left 2 !.model MYD2 D(Bv=3.3 type=zener)
TEXT 2312 2392 Left 2 ;ATT!!\nComment out CD components that I use that are not CD4013B from cd4000_v lib \nthis avoids problems from overlapping definitions
TEXT -1552 -296 Left 2 ;Flip flops' supply voltage (their models \nrequire a source called VDD\nThey will be supplied by VDDR in real life
TEXT -856 -336 Left 2 ;VDDR actually powers VDD of digital components\nWe don't power them directly from VDDRSIG because\nthe CD4000 power consumption values are off.
TEXT -2672 -2096 Left 5 ;Input power path
TEXT 1496 -2088 Left 5 ;Capdyn input control
TEXT -1832 -2080 Left 5 ;Capdyn: main bank and switches
TEXT -3688 -976 Left 5 ;Capdyn: automatic switching mechanism
TEXT 1488 -1608 Left 5 ;Capdyn output control
TEXT 1672 -944 Left 2 ;RC filter slows down Q1 signal with respect\n to UVB to trigger CLR upon desired action.
TEXT 3208 -296 Left 2 ;Next task config memorization is implemented in capdyn as a shift register, piloted directly by MCU.\nNot in use for this simulation, but implemented in capdynv2 PCB.\nSee full_v2 to see this part working as it does in the v2 PCB.\nIn v2 PCB, configuration is passed to capdyn's "next task" shift register through these signals:\nD_IN: data line with desired next stage information\nCLK_CNF: clock line for sampling data line, produced by MCU
TEXT -2664 -1440 Left 5 ;Output power path
TEXT 2296 2328 Left 5 ;Simulation parameters and libraries
TEXT 3584 -1368 Left 2 ;Instead of OVB_bck, which triggers task\njust when backup reaches 5.5 V \nwe can trigger task whenever\nbackup is in its voltage range by using EN1
TEXT 2240 -1744 Left 2 ;EN1_CAP triggered high when backup cap first charges up to 5.5V\ntriggered low when backup cap discharges down to 3.85 V (clamping diodes limit)
TEXT 1480 -1744 Left 2 ;Window type voltage monitor simulator:\nThis lower limit is dictated by clamping diodes at the switches
TEXT 1480 -656 Left 5 ;Capdyn configuration control
TEXT 3208 -1800 Left 2 ;INPUT power drive EN1:\nEN1_CAP signals that the backup capacitor is in its range, so capdyn can be safely charged.\nNOTRDY allows power to be redirected to the backup cap when the main bank is charged\nto the desired stage. EN2 allows capdyn to still accumulate energy when load is activated (EN2).
TEXT -2664 -536 Left 2 ;DLR: serial data to be shifted right\nIn our case, always VCC
TEXT -3656 -536 Left 2 ;Window type voltage monitor simulator\nupper switching threshold for capdyn: 3.65\nlower switching threshold for capdyn: 1.80
TEXT 3152 -904 Left 3 ;3. EN2_CAP adds additional conditions to capdyn's output activation.\nIt is set when both the main bank is ready (RDY) and the backup \ncapacitor has just reached 5.5 V (OVB_bck).\nIt is reset when either the MCU has finished its operations\nor if main bank becomes discharged (NOTRDY)
TEXT 3600 -1632 Left 2 ;* In previous versions (v3.1 downwards), instead of \ndetaching the load at UVB_BCK signal, we used a\n comparator to provide a PGOOD signal. \nThis allowed us to extend execution down to 3.1 V_BCK.
TEXT 2304 2584 Left 2 !.inc tlv3691.txt
TEXT 2304 -1632 Left 2 ;This part of the circuit controls output power flow from the main bank.\nOutput is activated when desired stage is fully charged and backup capacitor is topped up.\nOutput is deactivated if either we overdischarge bank or backup, or if we end operation on MCU side.\nA successfull task should only end due to this latter reason.
TEXT 2304 2656 Left 2 !.param cap=0.0060 decoup=5n pow_inp=10e-4
TEXT -1136 80 Left 5 ;This section is responsible for attaching to CapDYN a load consuming a certain amount of energy.\nTasks are configured as a constant power load for a certain amount of time, yielding the measured \ntask energy consumption in worst case scenario (max supply voltage)
TEXT 2616 2584 Left 2 !.ic V(V_cpdn)=0 V(pow_in)=0 V(VDDRSIG)=2.5 V(V_BCK)=0
TEXT -400 1152 Left 3 ;EOF simulates task duration by delaying POS_EDGE (EN_2 rising edge pulse) of the active task's duration. \nEOF must only be a short peak: if it stays high, we cannot set EN2 at capdyn load control side, as EOF keeps RDY in reset!
TEXT -2312 984 Left 3 ;EOFR clocks the next task shift register immediately after an EOF is issued (EN2 rising edge, delayed by task time)\nNext task register must be clocked once EOF has had its effect on capdyn output control (EN2).\nFor this purpose, we delay the EOF signal by 50 ms and pilot the task register with this delayed signal, EOFR.\n \nIf we were to pilot task register directly from EOF:\nEOF would not have time to deactivate EN2, causing problems.
TEXT 3320 -368 Left 5 ;Capdyn "next task" shift register
TEXT 1480 -216 Left 2 ;Ti signals tell capdyn at which stage completion (Qi) we want to turn on load output.\nIn PCB: Ti signal is output of next task shift register, which is piloted by D_IN and CLK_CNF of MCU.\nIn this simulation, Ti are produced through a simple task scheduler circuit acting as the MCU + some other logic acting as the next task register
TEXT -3672 128 Left 3 ;This acts as the MCU's task scheduling algorithm, which sets up a circular sequence of four tasks.\nOnce EN2 is enabled, the current task executed and issues an EOF at the end of the task. Once EOF is issued, we set up the next task.\nIn reality, the MCU will control the task register in capdyn, setting up the next task in the "next task" register.\nFor the sake of the LTspice simulation, we needed the MCu's signals telling us which is the next task - this is what we have done here,\nTo see how the task register is actually implemented, see full_v2
TEXT -3688 72 Left 5 ;TASK SCHEDULER (MCU-SIDE) + NEXT TASK SHIFT REGISTER (CAPDYN-SIDE) SIMULATION
TEXT -440 -992 Left 2 ;RC to filter the OVB peak when we go from stage 3 to 4 (spike of Vcpdn)\nAddition of AND gate after RC to have equal line switching characteristics and decoupling
TEXT 1632 392 Left 5 ;Next task shift register - capdyn side
TEXT -2056 488 Left 3 ;EN2 rising edge identifies moment where load is first connected to capdyn main bank.\nThis circuit generates a 100 ms pulse immediately as EN2 rising edge is detected.
TEXT -3680 384 Left 5 ;MCU simulation - next task register control
TEXT -536 392 Left 5 ;MCU simulation - load avg power and duration simulation
TEXT -504 488 Left 4 ;Task duration and avg power -> task energy requirements\nTask energy can be matched to capacitor dimensioning through the energy_check.m script\nRemember, if we only have energy requirements and no time for execution, longest time \nwill lead to increased energy being consumed by capdyn infrastructure.
TEXT -352 704 Left 3 ;Load average power consumption varying with matched stage.
TEXT -384 1072 Left 2 ;V=1*if(V(T1)>=0.1 | V(T2)>=0.1, delay(V(POS_EDGE), 1s),0)  + if(V(T3)>=0.1, delay(V(POS_EDGE), 4s),0) + if(V(T4)>=0.1, delay(V(POS_EDGE),15s),0)\nFor multiple tasks having same time
TEXT -3680 -1824 Left 2 ;Constant power input of pow_inp\nTo avoid division by zero we add 0.0000001 at the denominator.\nTo check stage-task matching under worst case assumption, \nwe assume no input power when load is active.\nTo  add a power failure and power recovery at 56 and 140 s:.\nI = if(time < 56 | time >140 , if(V(EN2)<0.1,{pow_inp}/(abs(V(pow_in)) + 0.1),0), 0)
TEXT -2008 -376 Left 2 ;Attach to V_BCK to \nsimulate backup failures
TEXT -2544 -1544 Left 2 ;D3 and D4 are SIP ideal diodes
TEXT -2544 -1512 Left 2 !.model SIP D(Ron=200e-3, Roff=4.3e7)
TEXT -3400 -312 Left 2 ;Behavioural models integration\nConsumption of 2 LTC2934\n + 2  res div (700nAx2)
TEXT -2816 -336 Left 2 ;Power consumption of all other digital components.
TEXT -2976 2384 Left 2 ;Accessible energy at each stage: 0.5*v_cpdn^2*C_stage
TEXT -2984 2080 Left 2 ;Accessible energy\nEnergy which can actually be seen from the V_CPDN output.
TEXT -1352 1880 Left 2 ;Full energy balance: e_in - e_out, including consumption of components and backup overhead.
TEXT -2968 1712 Left 2 ;Stored energy - sum of 0.5*cap*vcap^2\ntotal energy stored in the bank at a given instant.
TEXT -3168 2840 Left 2 ;Stored energy can be decomposed into accessible energy and trapped energy.
TEXT -1552 2552 Left 3 ;After a full task cycle, we care about the difference between E_in and E_out.\nOf course, we want to have E_in = E_out as much as it is possible. \nThat difference is given by \n* trapped charge\n* energy that is still accessible but below threshold\n* energy that is dissipated
TEXT -3696 1600 Left 5 ;ENERGY CONSIDERATIONS
TEXT -2168 1728 Left 5 ;STORED ENERGY
